\description{
Fits a linear model to every feature's log abundance, carrying the
uncertainty of low counts and zeros (Dirichlet posterior draws) and of
the samples' unmeasured total loads (a \strong{scale model}) through to the
estimates and p-values: the linear models of ALDEx3 (McGovern &
Silverman 2026; Nixon et al. 2025). It is the regression form of
\code{\link[=dirmult_ttest]{dirmult_ttest()}}: any design, with covariates, numeric variables and an
optional random intercept, and a scale model that can use measured loads.
}
\details{
With \eqn{x_{sf}} the abundance of feature \eqn{f} in sample \eqn{s},
each of the \code{n_draws} draws:
\enumerate{
\item Draws each sample's composition from its Dirichlet posterior,
\eqn{G_{sf} \sim \mathrm{Gamma}(x_{sf} +} \code{pseudocount}\eqn{, 1)}, and
takes \eqn{\log_2} of the proportions \eqn{G_{sf} / \sum_g G_{sg}}.
\item Draws each sample's log2 scale (total load) from the scale model, and
adds it: \eqn{Y_{sf} = \log_2 \mathrm{composition}_{sf} + \log_2
   \mathrm{scale}_s}, an estimate of the feature's log2 absolute
abundance.
\item Fits the linear model \eqn{Y_{\cdot f} = X\beta_f + e} to every
feature by least squares (or, with a random intercept, by REML), with
the \code{se_type} standard errors and t statistics.
}

\code{estimate} and \code{se} average the draws' coefficients and standard errors.
The p-values follow ALDEx3's \code{aldex.pvals()}, which allows for draws in
which a coefficient changes sign (Nixon et al. 2025): each draw's
one-sided p-values for a negative and for a positive coefficient,
doubled and capped at 1, are averaged over the draws separately, and
\code{p_value} is the smaller average. For \code{p_adjusted}, each draw's doubled
one-sided p-values are first adjusted across the features (with
\code{p_adjust}, separately for each design column), then averaged and the
smaller taken. So \code{p_adjusted} is an average of adjusted p-values, as in
ALDEx2 and ALDEx3, where \code{\link[=dirmult_ttest]{dirmult_ttest()}} adjusts its averaged
p-values, as scikit-bio does. A feature is rejected when \code{p_adjusted <= alpha}; there is no interval criterion (unlike \code{\link[=dirmult_ttest]{dirmult_ttest()}}), so
decisions settle as \code{n_draws} grows.

\strong{Scale models.} Sequencing gives each sample's composition, not its
total load (its scale), and a log fold change of absolute abundance is
a fold change of composition plus a fold change of load. Every
compositional method assumes something about the second term; a scale
model says what, and how sure that is (Nixon et al. 2025):\tabular{ll}{
   Information available \tab Scale model \cr
   The clr's centre (the geometric mean of the features) is plausible: most features don't change \tab \code{scale_model = 'clr'} (default), \code{scale_sd = 0.5} \cr
   Total load plausibly doesn't differ systematically between groups \tab \code{scale_model = 'tss'}, \code{scale_sd = 0.5} \cr
   Measured or predicted total load per sample (flow cytometry, qPCR, spike-ins, a load predictor) \tab \code{load}, with \code{load_sd} its error (log2) \cr
   Certainty in either assumption (a normalization, as in most methods) \tab \code{scale_sd = 0} \cr
}


With \code{'clr'}, each sample's log2 scale is minus its mean log2
proportion (the clr), plus \eqn{x_s^\top\Lambda}; with \code{'tss'}, it is
\eqn{x_s^\top\Lambda} alone; in both, \eqn{\Lambda \sim N(0,}
\code{scale_sd}\eqn{^2 I)} is drawn afresh in each draw, one value per design
column, so that samples with the same design share a shift and every
coefficient gets its own uncertainty, added to its estimate's spread over
the draws. With \code{load}, each sample's log2 scale is \eqn{N(\log_2}
\code{load}\eqn{_s,} \code{load_sd}\eqn{_s^2)}, independently per sample. A larger
\code{scale_sd} (or \code{load_sd}) widens the averaged p-values, so only changes
clear of plausible changes in load are rejected; it controls false
discoveries when the assumed scale is wrong, at a cost in power (Nixon et
al. 2025; Dos Santos et al. 2026). Check how the results depend on it:
\code{\link[=da_sensitivity]{da_sensitivity()}} with \code{scale_sds = TRUE} reruns the model at
\code{scale_sd = 0}, \code{0.25}, \code{0.5} and \code{1}. With a scale model, use many
draws: the p-values average over the rare large shifts.

\strong{Units.} Every estimate is in log2 units of absolute abundance, as the
scale model defines it (the \code{log_base} attribute). With \code{'clr'} and
\code{scale_sd = 0} the coefficients are those of a regression of each
feature's clr values (log2); the intercept is then a feature's mean clr
value in the reference samples. With \code{load}, they are log2 changes of
absolute abundance; the unit of \code{load} shifts only the intercept.

\strong{Relation to \code{\link[=dirmult_ttest]{dirmult_ttest()}}.} For the same \code{seed} the two functions
draw the same Gamma variables for a sample (one random stream per draw
and sample of the table), so with \code{scale_model = 'clr'}, \code{scale_sd = 0}
and \code{~ group} for two groups, \code{estimate} for the group's column is
\code{dirmult_ttest()}'s \code{log2_fc} for \verb{contrast = c(<second group>, <first group>)} (to rounding), and \code{resid_sd} and \code{mean} are its \code{pooled_sd}
and \code{mean}. The tests differ: an OLS t-test with HC3 standard errors here
(\code{se_type = 'classical'} gives the pooled-variance t-test), Welch's
t-test there; and their scale models draw different shifts (one per
design column here, one for the contrast there).

\strong{Repeated measures.} With a random intercept, \code{~ status + (1 | subject)}, each draw's model is a linear mixed model, fitted by REML,
with Satterthwaite's degrees of freedom: ALDEx3's \code{method = "lme4"},
whose fits use lme4 and lmerTest (McGovern & Silverman 2026), with
ecodive's REML fit instead (as \code{\link[=linda]{linda()}}'s): one random intercept, of a
column of \code{data}, with at least two values and fewer values than
samples; random slopes and several random terms are not supported.
ecodive finds the exact REML optimum, where lme4's optimizer stops close
to it, so results agree with ALDEx3's to about \code{1e-5}. A mixed model
takes longer: about 0.1 ms of CPU time per feature and draw for 100
samples in 25 groups, where a linear model takes a few microseconds.

\strong{Matching ALDEx3.} Given the same Dirichlet and scale draws, the
results are those of ALDEx3 1.4.0's \code{aldex()} (\code{method = "lm"}, \code{test = "t.HC3"}, \code{"t.HC0"} or \code{"t"}) and \code{summary()} to about \code{1e-12}: the
tests feed both the same draws. The draws themselves come from ecodive's
own samplers (on PCG, not R's \code{rgamma()} and \code{rnorm()}), so for the same
data the results differ from ALDEx3's by Monte Carlo error only.
Differences from ALDEx3: least squares by a QR decomposition, not the
normal equations (more accurate for ill-conditioned designs); the
defaults \code{p_adjust = 'holm'} (ALDEx3: \code{'BH'}) and \code{n_draws = 1000}
(2,000); ALDEx3 needs a scale model to be named, here \code{'clr'} with
\code{scale_sd = 0.5} is the default; the intercept's rows are kept (ALDEx3's
\code{summary()} drops them); a rank-deficient design is an error (as in
ALDEx3, where \code{solve()} fails); with HC3 standard errors, a sample of
leverage 1 (the only sample of a group) makes the standard errors and
p-values \code{NaN}, with a warning, where ALDEx3 gives meaningless numbers;
ALDEx3's \code{coefficient.sm()} (a prior on each coefficient's shift),
custom scale functions, correlated load errors (\code{s.cov}), one-sided
tests, the \code{nlme} and \code{blmm} mixed-model engines and the effect-size
diagnostics of \code{aldex.effect()} are not offered; and there is no
streaming, as no draws are kept.

\strong{Computation.} The draws run in parallel C, one at a time, with memory
for one draw (8 bytes per value of the table); every sample of every
draw has its own random stream, so results are identical for any number
of \code{cpus}. The p-values' adjustment calls \code{\link[stats:p.adjust]{stats::p.adjust()}} for each
draw and covariate.
}
\section{Same names in other packages}{

ALDEx3's \code{aldex()} is the original method (see \strong{Matching ALDEx3}).
ALDEx2's \code{aldex.glm()} (Bioconductor) also fits a linear model to each
clr draw, without a scale model by default: with \code{scale_sd = 0} and
\code{se_type = 'classical'}, its estimates and standard errors agree with
this function's to Monte Carlo error (its p-values are averages of the
draws' p-values, not ALDEx3's aggregation of their two tails).
}

\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\examples{
    # Two groups and a numeric covariate.
    set.seed(1)
    x <- matrix(rpois(16 * 20, 50), 16, 20, dimnames = list(NULL, paste0('f', 1:20)))
    x[9:16, 1:3] <- x[9:16, 1:3] * 4
    meta <- data.frame(trt = rep(c('control', 'treated'), each = 8), age = 21:36)

    res <- dirmult_lm(x, ~ trt + age, meta, n_draws = 200)
    res
    plot(res)

    # Measured loads, with a log2 error of 0.25.
    meta$load <- c(rep(1e9, 8), rep(2e9, 8))
    dirmult_lm(x, ~ trt, meta, load = 'load', load_sd = 0.25, n_draws = 200)

    # Which decisions hold over a range of scale uncertainties.
    sens <- da_sensitivity(x, 'dirmult_lm', 'trt', meta, scale_sds = TRUE, n_draws = 100)
    plot(sens, 'traces')

}
